From f02b920e76e68cfdc38dd32117f9cbad672f38b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 21:15:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: keep a queued access unit of the layer in one
 entry

An access unit of the layer that waited to be given to its decoder was
kept in seven arrays side by side (its packet, picture order, time from
the stream, verdict, RPU, carrier and whether its RPU can carry a
residual), moved one by one in three places: every new field had to be
remembered in each of them. It is now one entry (struct queued_au), moved
once. The marks of access units that are not decoded take the base
decoder's reading of their RPU with ring_find() and metadata_frame(), as
the other answers do, instead of a copy of both; the RPU of a queued access
unit is taken with layer_rpu(), which also took it written out in place;
the ring is looked up with ring_find() when a base picture brings its RPU.
The decoder context that dvbridge_fel_create() prepares says why it stays
(the host tests open it in software; Kodi opens one of its own on the
device of the base decoder).

Nothing that the module does changes: on the host, the pictures (and the
metadata each one goes out with) of fel_rpu_test (as Kodi: the whole FEL
test stream, with start codes, with a seek, two starts in the middle, a
switch to another display, an RPU taken out of one access unit in seven,
an RPU that FFmpeg refuses, a fixed pool smaller than the layer needs; the
file with its layers swapped, whole and from packet 20), of mel_test (five
schedules, one with a seek, and the two-track transport stream) and of
fel_tracks_test (the two-track Matroska and transport stream files, and
the starts at each random-access picture) are byte for byte those of
before, with the sanitizers of address and undefined behaviour.
